  "factCheck": {
    "status": "verified",
    "by": "HeyMariner daily desk",
    "checkedAt": "2026-11-16",
    "notes": "marinediesels.info: causes = combustible oil in the scavenge space (cylinder oil draining down, crankcase oil carried up through a faulty stuffing box), plentiful scavenge air, heat from piston blow-by; symptoms = high exhaust temperature of affected units, power loss, turbocharger surging, hot spots on the scavenge trunk, sparks/smoke from scavenge drains; actions = dead slow ahead, fuel taken off affected cylinders, increase cylinder lubrication, shut scavenge drains; major fire: stop engine, engage turning gear, extinguishing medium (CO2, dry powder or steam) through scavenge trunk fittings; keep clear of relief valves and do not open up until the engine has cooled; prevention = clean drains, piston ring maintenance, no excess cylinder oil. Exact procedure and fixed extinguishing medium depend on the engine maker manual and the company SMS."
  },
